{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778193230,"nonce":"k59KXp0fGB4ERW3pZBSwKV5Wk58=","title":"test_simplefeedback_runs fails on develop base (pre-existing; unrelated to Graph.step PR)","message":"**Surface.** `tests/test_components.py::test_simplefeedback_runs` fails on `develop` independently of any feature branch. Reproduces with worktree on `develop` directly (no `feature/graph-public-step` changes applied).\n\n**Discovery context.** Surfaced while implementing `Graph.step` (`0ec8492`/`d7616e0`) — the test failure was observed in the feature worktree. To verify it wasn't caused by the Graph.step changes, the subagent stashed the branch's changes and re-ran the test on the develop base; failure reproduced. So this is a pre-existing bug in develop, not a regression from the new public step API.\n\n**Why this matters.** `SimpleFeedback` is the canonical sensorimotor-loop graph in feedbax; rlrmp depends on it for all trained-model usage. If `test_simplefeedback_runs` is failing, real `SimpleFeedback` instances may be partially broken on develop in ways downstream users (rlrmp included) don't notice until they hit the failing path.\n\n**Failure mode** (per the subagent's diagnostic): cycle-init missing for `feedback.mechanics`. Likely candidate: somewhere in the cycle-port initial-value resolution chain, the `feedback` node doesn't get a default value for its `mechanics` input port despite the cycle wire spec. Could be a regression from a recent develop commit or a long-standing latent bug.\n\n**Action:** investigate, fix, add a regression test that explicitly exercises a SimpleFeedback rollout on the develop base — not as a smoke test that happens to pass when the cycle-init machinery works, but as a deliberate \"real SimpleFeedback rolls out without errors\" test.","files":null}]}